 "title": "Храбрый круассан  (The Brave Croissant)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "max": "Макс",
  "vika": "Вика"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max goes to the bar, where Vika has poured him a drink to say thanks. They talk about whether he is a croissant or a stick of celery. Here is the scene in Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А я кто?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему круассан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я же не ем мучное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты спас наш ресторан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты молодец, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я просто сделал свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Нет, это было круто.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты очень помог мне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Спасибо тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мой храбрый круассан.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go through it again, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А я кто?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what am I?' 'A ya kto' is 'and I who', with no verb. The little 'a' at the start is the 'and' that starts a new thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A croissant or celery?' Both 'kruassan' and 'selderey' are loan words, so you can guess them. 'Ili' means 'or'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, of course a croissant.' Vika answers without a verb, just 'kruassan'. 'Nu' is a 'well' that makes it sound casual.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему круассан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why a croissant?' Max repeats her answer as a question. It works like a mini echo.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я же не ем мучное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I don't eat flour products.' 'Muchnoe' means 'floury things' and grows from 'muka', flour. 'Ne em' is 'I don't eat', and 'zhe' says 'after all'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты спас наш ресторан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You saved our restaurant.' 'Spas' is 'saved', and 'nash restoran' stays the same because it is the thing saved. A short, grateful sentence.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты молодец, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You did great, Max.' 'Molodets' is 'good lad', and it's for men. For a woman Russians would say 'molodets' too.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я просто сделал свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I just did my job.' 'Svoyu rabotu' is 'my own job', with 'u' endings. Max is modest, or tries to be.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Нет, это было круто.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, it was cool.' 'Kruto' means 'cool' in slang, and it literally means 'steeply'. 'Eto bylo' is 'this was'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты очень помог мне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You helped me a lot.' 'Pomog mne' is 'helped to me', so the helped person goes in the 'to' form. 'Ochen' is 'very'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Спасибо тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you.' 'Spasibo tebe' is 'thanks to you', and the 'tebe' makes it personal. It's what you'd say to a friend.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мой храбрый круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'My brave croissant.' 'Khrabry' means 'brave', and it describes the croissant. A pet name, nothing more. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
